# macOS
.DS_Store

# Claude Code. Everything here is machine-local EXCEPT the plugin install
# reference, which is governance state (#833). `isPluginGoverned()` reads
# `enabledPlugins`; `extraKnownMarketplaces` is load-bearing for a different
# reason — without it the plugin never resolves, which `_isCompletePluginRef`
# is the contract for — so both halves are committed. A clone without the
# reference reads as ungoverned, which switches CLAUDE.md's merge strategy from
# managed-block to WHOLE-FILE and destroys it on first launch. Tracking this
# file is what makes a committed CLAUDE.md survive.
#
# `settings.local.json` (permissions) stays ignored: genuinely per-machine.
#
# The `hooks` block is NOT committed. It carries absolute paths to one machine's
# checkout, and a clone elsewhere would run two commands that do not exist at
# every Claude Code start — a broken hook, not merely a stale path. `syncEngineHooks`
# writes it at launch, so **expect this tracked file to show as modified**; that is
# known, and moving machine-local keys out for good is #1242.
.claude/*
!.claude/settings.json

# Prawduct dogfooding state (TC builds itself with Prawduct).
# Contents are ignored fail-closed; only the change-log is tracked — it feeds
# the check-change-log-entry PR gate and the release flow (#485).
#
# `backlog.md` is tracked for the same reason `.tangleclaw/plans/` is (see below):
# an untracked backlog is one machine's working tree, so it does not survive a
# fresh clone and is not in any export. That matters most at the GitHub-Issues
# cutover — once `backlog_service_repo` is set the skill treats this file as
# frozen history, and archived items that were never migrated exist ONLY here.
# Ignoring it would make every clone of this repo backlog-less.
.prawduct/*
!.prawduct/change-log.md
!.prawduct/backlog.md

# The migration audit trail — the decisions, the restructure plan, and the
# before/after preview the owner approved. Scoped to these three files rather
# than `!.prawduct/artifacts/`, which would publish ~54 internal design docs
# (~1.3 MB, incl. the security model) to a PUBLIC repo — a separate decision,
# deliberately not taken here.
!.prawduct/artifacts/
.prawduct/artifacts/*
!.prawduct/artifacts/migration-scrub-decisions.md
!.prawduct/artifacts/migration-restructure-plan.json
!.prawduct/artifacts/migration-restructure-preview.md

# TangleClaw project config (dogfooding artifact).
# Contents are ignored fail-closed, with ONE exception: `plans/` is tracked.
# A plan is project state, not machine state — it must survive a fresh clone and
# an engine switch, and the archiving convention explicitly leans on that ("the
# issue-state check is the contract — it protects across fresh clones, which have
# no local archive"). Ignoring plans made every clone of this repo planless.
# `.tangleclaw/*` rather than `.tangleclaw/`: git never descends into an ignored
# DIRECTORY, so a `!` negation below a bare `.tangleclaw/` would never match.
.tangleclaw/*
!.tangleclaw/plans/

# Priming prompts, for the same reason and by the same mechanism. The Global
# Rules say a recurring session role's prompt is "a durable artifact, not chat
# memory" and name this path as git-tracked — but `.tangleclaw/*` above swallowed
# the directory, so the rule had been quietly unfollowable: `roadmap-triage.md`
# sat untracked from the day it was written, invisible to a fresh clone and to
# every review.
!.tangleclaw/priming/

# Engine configs TangleClaw GENERATES when its own clone is attached as a managed
# project (#708 — the first-run scan detects the clone and pre-selects it). These
# are machine-generated, not source, and an untracked one here is not cosmetic: it
# makes the working tree dirty, which trips `lib/update-applier.js`'s dirty-tree
# guard and blocks the self-updater. A first-time installer hit exactly that —
# TangleClaw generated `.codex.yaml` into its own clone, and the resulting dirty
# tree stranded them on the version containing the bug they were working around.
#
# CLAUDE.md is deliberately NOT listed: it is tracked (#833). TangleClaw does
# write to it — since #1021 it splices its own BEGIN/END:tangleclaw block on
# every launch — but only inside those markers, so the hand-authored content
# around them survives. Expect it to show as modified: the generated block is
# rendered from this machine's state (the API base URL carries the live port,
# the Medusa routes carry the project name), so a clone on a different port
# dirties it at first launch exactly as settings.json does. Same follow-up,
# #1242. A regenerated CLAUDE.md used to strand the self-updater too; #1241
# fixed that WITHOUT widening the discard set — the updater discards this file
# only when it differs from the committed copy solely inside those markers, so a
# hand edit outside them still blocks the update, exactly as before.
.codex.yaml
.aider.conf.yml

